          "type": "turn",
          "question": "No chest pain at all, but I do get a bit puffed walking up the hill lately. Is that just age?",
          "answer": "Becoming puffed or out of breath when walking uphill can occur for several reasons. Commonly, it may be due to physical deconditioning, where lower fitness levels result in reduced stamina during physical exertion. As people age, physical activity often decreases, contributing to this reduced capacity for exercise.\n\nAge can impact breathing and overall exertion levels as well. As we age, we may experience natural changes in lung function and muscle strength, which can affect our ability to perform activities like walking uphill. Conditions such as chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) or cardiovascular issues may also become more prevalent with age, leading to breathlessness during exertion (source: General clinical reference, COPD).\n\nIf your experience of becoming puffed is new and noticeable, it might be worth discussing with a clinician to evaluate your overall health, especially if it affects your daily activities.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
